1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,760
In a laboratory in Boulder, Colorado,
there is a clock whose frequency

2
00:00:03,990 --> 00:00:08,940
is known so precisely that even over
the entire age of the universe,

3
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:11,900
the uncertainty would add up
to less than a second.

4
00:00:12,276 --> 00:00:14,256
And it isn't the end of the road.

5
00:00:14,636 --> 00:00:18,036
Every generation of clocks
has been better than the last.

6
00:00:18,474 --> 00:00:20,614
So here's the obvious question.

7
00:00:21,054 --> 00:00:22,594
How far can this go?

8
00:00:23,265 --> 00:00:27,145
Is there a point where the problem
isn't the clock any more, but time itself?

9
00:00:28,569 --> 00:00:32,089
In classical physics,
time was treated as perfectly sharp.

10
00:00:32,729 --> 00:00:34,489
Only our clocks were clumsy.

11
00:00:35,259 --> 00:00:39,099
But in 2025, a group of physicists
in Rome and Budapest

12
00:00:39,329 --> 00:00:42,799
published a calculation
that puts a crack in that assumption.

13
00:00:43,326 --> 00:00:46,646
If one particular idea
about quantum mechanics is right,

14
00:00:46,726 --> 00:00:51,286
then every clock in the universe
picks up a tiny, random uncertainty.

15
00:00:51,977 --> 00:00:53,917
Not because it's badly built.

16
00:00:53,997 --> 00:00:57,337
Because, in these models,
time itself would jitter.

17
00:00:58,341 --> 00:01:00,761
And there's a twist
nobody would have guessed.

18
00:01:01,488 --> 00:01:03,908
The clocks that suffer most
aren't the cheap ones.

19
00:01:03,988 --> 00:01:06,148
They're the smallest ones.

20
00:01:07,748 --> 00:01:11,448
To see why, we need three pieces.
How a clock works.

21
00:01:11,828 --> 00:01:13,588
What gravity does to time.

22
00:01:13,688 --> 00:01:17,628
And a very old problem
at the heart of quantum mechanics.

23
00:01:19,328 --> 00:01:21,708
A clock doesn't measure time directly.

24
00:01:22,308 --> 00:01:24,128
It counts something that repeats.

25
00:01:24,994 --> 00:01:26,394
A pendulum swings.

26
00:01:26,474 --> 00:01:28,954
A quartz crystal vibrates.

27
00:01:29,508 --> 00:01:33,868
The clock in Boulder counts the waves
of a laser locked to strontium atoms.

28
00:01:34,888 --> 00:01:38,368
About four hundred and twenty-nine
trillion of them every second.

29
00:01:38,993 --> 00:01:41,733
Count enough repetitions,
and you have a second.

30
00:01:42,353 --> 00:01:45,153
The more steadily the thing repeats,
the better the clock.

31
00:01:47,483 --> 00:01:50,183
And this is where gravity
enters the story.

32
00:01:50,564 --> 00:01:54,004
Einstein showed that clocks
don't all run at the same rate.

33
00:01:54,644 --> 00:01:59,464
A clock deeper in a gravitational field
ticks more slowly than a clock higher up.

34
00:01:59,663 --> 00:02:04,683
And we can measure it. In 2010,
physicists raised one atomic clock

35
00:02:04,913 --> 00:02:08,523
by just thirty-three centimetres,
and saw it run faster.

36
00:02:09,097 --> 00:02:12,337
In 2022, the team in Boulder went further.

37
00:02:12,817 --> 00:02:17,257
They saw the difference inside a single
cloud of atoms, across one millimetre.

38
00:02:18,369 --> 00:02:21,389
The top of the cloud
aged faster than the bottom.

39
00:02:22,069 --> 00:02:24,589
By about one part in ten billion billion.

40
00:02:25,272 --> 00:02:28,352
So the rate of a clock
depends on the gravity around it.

41
00:02:28,612 --> 00:02:31,152
And if gravity itself fluctuated,
at random,

42
00:02:31,382 --> 00:02:35,892
in the way some quantum models predict,
time would fluctuate with it.

43
00:02:38,322 --> 00:02:40,202
Why would gravity fluctuate?

44
00:02:40,842 --> 00:02:43,862
The answer comes
from that very old quantum problem.

45
00:02:44,321 --> 00:02:48,001
In quantum mechanics, a particle
doesn't have to be in one place.

46
00:02:48,501 --> 00:02:50,181
It can be in a superposition.

47
00:02:50,261 --> 00:02:53,101
Here and there, at the same time.

48
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:57,780
The equations of quantum mechanics
let that superposition go on forever.

49
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:00,140
They never pick an answer.

50
00:03:00,475 --> 00:03:04,695
And yet, whenever we look,
we see one answer. Here. Or there.

51
00:03:04,925 --> 00:03:06,125
Never both.

52
00:03:06,477 --> 00:03:09,477
What makes nature choose
is called the measurement problem.

53
00:03:10,297 --> 00:03:14,037
And after a hundred years,
physicists still disagree about it.

54
00:03:15,154 --> 00:03:17,974
One family of answers
is called collapse models.

55
00:03:18,854 --> 00:03:23,234
They say nature makes the choice itself,
all the time, with nobody watching.

56
00:03:23,892 --> 00:03:25,592
In the first complete collapse model,

57
00:03:25,822 --> 00:03:29,592
from the 1980s,
a single particle gets pinned down

58
00:03:29,822 --> 00:03:32,332
about once every three hundred
million years.

59
00:03:32,691 --> 00:03:36,351
But a cat is made of roughly
a billion billion billion particles.

60
00:03:37,151 --> 00:03:41,491
Together, they get pinned down
around a hundred billion times a second.

61
00:03:42,133 --> 00:03:47,113
In these models, that's why atoms can be
in two places at once, and cats can't.

62
00:03:49,573 --> 00:03:51,233
Now the gravity part.

63
00:03:51,933 --> 00:03:57,053
Roger Penrose and Lajos Diósi suggested
where these collapses might come from.

64
00:03:57,585 --> 00:04:02,525
A mass in two places at once means two
different gravitational fields at once.

65
00:04:03,164 --> 00:04:06,404
Penrose argued that nature
can't keep that up for long.

66
00:04:07,084 --> 00:04:08,764
Gravity forces the choice.

67
00:04:09,377 --> 00:04:13,077
And the 2025 paper,
with Diósi as one of its authors,

68
00:04:13,307 --> 00:04:15,317
makes a surprising connection.

69
00:04:16,171 --> 00:04:17,871
The random noise in collapse models

70
00:04:18,101 --> 00:04:21,771
can be interpreted
as random fluctuations of gravity.

71
00:04:22,182 --> 00:04:24,402
And that works not just
for Penrose's model,

72
00:04:24,482 --> 00:04:26,222
which was already tied to gravity,

73
00:04:26,462 --> 00:04:29,502
but also for the most studied one,
which never was.

74
00:04:32,222 --> 00:04:34,162
Now put the pieces together.

75
00:04:34,835 --> 00:04:36,815
Gravity sets the rate of a clock.

76
00:04:37,315 --> 00:04:40,875
If gravity fluctuates,
the rate of every clock fluctuates too.

77
00:04:41,555 --> 00:04:46,095
A tiny bit faster.
A tiny bit slower. At random.

78
00:04:46,175 --> 00:04:49,895
And how much, as we'll see,
depends on the size of the clock.

79
00:04:50,577 --> 00:04:53,257
This doesn't mean time comes
in little pixels.

80
00:04:54,117 --> 00:04:57,037
The models don't predict
a smallest possible tick.

81
00:04:57,693 --> 00:05:02,493
It's more like a walk where every step
is a tiny bit off, in a random direction.

82
00:05:03,293 --> 00:05:05,013
Each step barely matters.

83
00:05:05,553 --> 00:05:07,853
But the steps don't cancel out completely.

84
00:05:08,613 --> 00:05:10,513
The uncertainty piles up.

85
00:05:11,073 --> 00:05:14,613
Run the clock four times longer,
and its typical drift doubles.

86
00:05:15,351 --> 00:05:18,291
Even a flawless clock
would pick up this uncertainty.

87
00:05:19,131 --> 00:05:22,531
No colder lab, no better engineering,
would remove it.

88
00:05:23,686 --> 00:05:27,926
And here's the twist.
Shielding doesn't matter. Size does.

89
00:05:28,984 --> 00:05:32,064
In the most studied collapse model,
the fluctuations change

90
00:05:32,294 --> 00:05:35,684
from place to place
over about one ten-millionth of a metre.

91
00:05:36,684 --> 00:05:38,704
Roughly the size of a virus.

92
00:05:39,429 --> 00:05:42,449
A clock that small
feels the full fluctuation.

93
00:05:42,909 --> 00:05:47,649
A bigger clock spans many places at once,
each with a slightly different change.

94
00:05:48,289 --> 00:05:49,949
They mostly average out.

95
00:05:50,301 --> 00:05:52,141
And a pulsar, a spinning dead star

96
00:05:52,371 --> 00:05:56,001
twenty kilometres across,
would be almost completely immune.

97
00:05:56,712 --> 00:06:00,412
In Penrose's version, the scale
is about a hundred times smaller.

98
00:06:00,492 --> 00:06:02,612
But the pattern holds.

99
00:06:03,172 --> 00:06:05,332
Bigger clocks average more of it away.

100
00:06:05,985 --> 00:06:09,765
So the most fundamentally uncertain clock
isn't the cheapest one.

101
00:06:10,285 --> 00:06:11,845
It's the smallest one.

102
00:06:14,445 --> 00:06:16,105
So how big is the drift?

103
00:06:17,595 --> 00:06:20,975
With the standard settings
of the most studied collapse model,

104
00:06:21,215 --> 00:06:23,795
a clock of the size
where the effect is strongest,

105
00:06:24,195 --> 00:06:26,755
running for a whole year,
would pick up an uncertainty

106
00:06:26,985 --> 00:06:29,855
of about ten to the minus twenty-eight
seconds.

107
00:06:30,577 --> 00:06:34,257
That's a ten-thousandth of a trillionth
of a trillionth of a second.

108
00:06:35,037 --> 00:06:37,217
Other settings
that experiments still allow

109
00:06:37,447 --> 00:06:41,717
could make it a few hundred times larger,
or a hundred times smaller.

110
00:06:42,787 --> 00:06:45,967
Today's best optical clocks
have ordinary timing fluctuations

111
00:06:46,197 --> 00:06:51,427
of around ten to the minus seventeen
seconds after just one second.

112
00:06:52,056 --> 00:06:53,756
Over a day, their noise is about

113
00:06:53,986 --> 00:06:57,416
a million billion times larger
than the predicted effect.

114
00:06:58,168 --> 00:06:59,948
Even a clock that had been running

115
00:07:00,028 --> 00:07:01,768
since the Big Bang
would have picked up only

116
00:07:01,998 --> 00:07:04,468
a few trillionths
of a trillionth of a second.

117
00:07:04,812 --> 00:07:09,272
And Penrose's version predicts a drift
a few hundred times smaller still.

118
00:07:09,636 --> 00:07:11,256
So the authors are clear.

119
00:07:11,956 --> 00:07:15,356
For every clock that exists,
this effect is completely negligible.

120
00:07:18,336 --> 00:07:20,316
Which raises a fair question.

121
00:07:20,996 --> 00:07:23,776
If no clock today can measure it,
why does it matter?

122
00:07:24,293 --> 00:07:26,813
Because it isn't really
a question about clocks.

123
00:07:27,453 --> 00:07:31,413
It's a question about whether
quantum mechanics is the whole story.

124
00:07:31,807 --> 00:07:36,107
If collapse models are wrong, this source
of uncertainty simply disappears.

125
00:07:36,580 --> 00:07:38,420
If they're right,
there's a fundamental limit

126
00:07:38,650 --> 00:07:41,200
to how precisely any clock can track time.

127
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:45,420
Not set by engineers. Set by nature.

128
00:07:47,770 --> 00:07:50,590
And collapse models
can be tested in other ways.

129
00:07:51,610 --> 00:07:55,510
In 2020, researchers working
deep beneath a mountain in Italy

130
00:07:55,740 --> 00:07:59,490
ruled out the simplest version
of the Diósi–Penrose model,

131
00:07:59,570 --> 00:08:01,650
the one with no adjustable settings.

132
00:08:02,353 --> 00:08:04,553
Other versions remain possible.

133
00:08:05,013 --> 00:08:07,313
And the experiments keep getting better.

134
00:08:10,013 --> 00:08:12,673
So, how precise can time actually be?

135
00:08:14,273 --> 00:08:16,273
Maybe not infinitely precise.

136
00:08:16,964 --> 00:08:21,404
And if that's true, then even a perfect
clock is drifting away from something.

137
00:08:22,567 --> 00:08:24,427
So here's a question for you.

138
00:08:24,947 --> 00:08:28,927
If a flawless clock still drifts,
what exactly is it drifting away from?

139
00:08:29,207 --> 00:08:30,677
Let me know in the comments.
